<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Unique Facts About My Area – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Unique Facts About My Area</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> 
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Create instructions for a task in personal life. Interactive: Compile feedback for collaborative writing in a structured manner.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write instructions for visiting a local attraction (e.g., "To reach the museum, walk two blocks from the bus station").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can compile and organize peer suggestions into a cohesive guide for must-see locations, adding personal insights and recommendations (e.g., "Several classmates recommended Casco Viejo for its historical significance, and I agree because its restored buildings offer a glimpse into Panama's past.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can provide a written response to a friend's question about the best places to visit (e.g., "In the past, I visited the old town, and it was an unforgettable experience. I know that today, there are more restaurants and walking areas, making it even more enjoyable for visitors.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students individually write down 2-3 unique attractions or locations in their local area that a visitor "must-see" or "should visit," along with one adjective to describe each (e.g., "Cerro Ancón - breathtaking views," "Biomuseo - interesting history"). They then quickly share their ideas with a partner. This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary (nouns: area, location, attraction; adjectives: breathtaking, interesting, must-see) and prompts quick written output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write simple directions and descriptions for a local landmark using prepositions of place and direction. For example, "To reach the Metropolitan Park, go straight for two blocks from the bus stop, then turn right. It's opposite the bank. The park showcases a unique variety of flora and fauna." The teacher writes this example on the board, pointing out the use of verbs like 'go,' 'turn,' and prepositions 'straight,' 'right,' 'opposite'. The teacher also demonstrates how to contrast past and present using "was once... but now it has become..." and how to use future with "will" and "going to" in recommendations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'go straight,' do you make any turns?" (Expected answer: "No, you keep going forward.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean if something is 'opposite' another building?" (Expected answer: "It's directly across from it.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The market was once small, but it has become very popular,' what tense do I use to talk about the past state?" (Expected answer: "Past simple.") "And for the current state?" (Expected answer: "Present perfect.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, authentic blog post excerpt or a section from a digital tourist guide describing "Three Must-See Locations in Casco Viejo" including directions, brief historical facts (using past simple/present perfect), and recommendations (using future 'will' and 'going to'). The text also includes reported speech where locals recommend places. (e.g., "Locals often say that the best coffee shops are found on Calle San Felipe.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing popular landmarks. Participating in group planning for events or trips, offering directions and suggestions clearly, ensuring everyone understands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text individually. Then, they complete the following written tasks:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Written response):</b> "What is the main purpose of this guide?" and "Which area of Panama is being described?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Written response):</b> Students match specific attractions (e.g., "National Theatre," "Plaza Mayor") from the text to their corresponding unique facts or directions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Written response):</b> Students read 3-4 statements about the text and write "True" or "False," correcting the false ones. For example: "The guide states that the seafood market was always a popular destination." (False: "The guide mentions it has become popular after recent renovations.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Target grammar: Past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions over time; Future with "will" and "going to" for intentions; Reported speech. Vocabulary: nouns (visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, town, history, blocks, cathedral, accommodations, landmark, guide, itinerary, precaution, safety, transportation), verbs (see, visit, plan, stay, explore, showcase, witness, connect, miss, recommend, give, explain, guide, describe, enjoy, navigate, direct, advise, arrange), adjectives (breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, unique, wonderful, must-see, local, national, famous, close, available, safe), prepositions (go straight, turn right, turn left, across from, next to, between, behind, opposite, in front of).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Transformation (5 min):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences, practicing target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part A: Past Simple vs. Present Perfect.</b> Complete sentences using the correct tense: "The old town (be) ________ once deserted, but now it (become) ________ a popular attraction." "In the past, visitors (not have) ________ many options, but recently new restaurants (open) ________."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part B: Reported Speech.</b> Rewrite direct speech as reported speech: "A friend said, 'Visit the Biomuseo!'" → "My friend advised me to visit the Biomuseo." "They told us, 'You will enjoy the seafood market.'" → "They said that we would enjoy the seafood market."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Direction Writing (5 min):</b> The teacher provides a simple map of a fictional "Local Attractions" area with 3-4 landmarks (e.g., Museum, Park, Market, Restaurant). Students choose two starting points and two destinations and write clear, step-by-step directions using at least three different prepositions of direction and two different verbs of movement from the vocabulary list. They also include one recommendation using "will" or "going to." (e.g., "From the Park, go straight for one block. Turn left at the bookstore. The museum is next to the cafe. You will really enjoy the exhibitions inside.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Writing: Describing a Landmark (5 min):</b> In pairs, students choose one unique local landmark (e.g., "a famous beach," "a historical cathedral," "an interesting market"). They collaboratively write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) describing it, using at least one past simple/present perfect contrast, one descriptive adjective, and one noun from the target vocabulary. (e.g., "Taboga Island was once a simple fishing town, but it has become a tranquil beach destination. Its breathtaking views make it a must-see location. We are going to plan a boat trip there soon.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative written guide / Instructional response to a friend.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Individual Draft (8 min):</b> Students imagine a friend is visiting their country and has asked for advice on what to see and do in their local area. Each student writes a short email or message (approx. 80-100 words) to their friend. The message should include:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recommendations for 1-2 unique local attractions, describing them with adjectives and historical facts (using past simple/present perfect).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear, simple instructions/directions on how to get to one of these attractions from a common starting point (e.g., "the main bus station").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one suggestion for a future activity they can do together, using "will" or "going to."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reference to what "they said" or "people recommend" (reported speech) regarding another aspect (e.g., "They said that the best seafood is found in the seafood market.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Feedback (5 min):</b> Students exchange their written messages with a partner. Each student reads their partner's message and provides written feedback using a simple checklist provided by the teacher (e.g., "Are the directions clear? Is reported speech used correctly? Is the vocabulary appropriate?"). They specifically look for clarity in instructions, correct use of grammar, and relevant vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Revision (2 min):</b> Students take back their original message and review the feedback. They make quick revisions based on their partner's suggestions, focusing on improving clarity and accuracy in their writing. This directly addresses the objective of compiling feedback for collaborative writing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during peer feedback and revision, and collection of revised written messages for review. A simple rubric will be used for grading the final written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their revised written message/guide to their friend. The teacher will focus on their ability to create clear, coherent instructions and descriptions, effectively incorporating the target grammar and vocabulary. The compilation of peer feedback for revision is also assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric categories):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Coherence (Writing):</b> Instructions are easy to follow, and descriptions are clear and logical.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Accurate application of past simple/present perfect, future 'will'/'going to', and reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Richness and Accuracy:</b> Effective use of a range of target nouns, verbs, adjectives, and prepositions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> All parts of the prompt (recommendations, directions, future plans, reported speech) are addressed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Incorporation of Feedback:</b> Evidence of revisions based on peer suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on an index card:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you learned or improved about writing instructions or recommendations today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How helpful was receiving feedback from your partner for your writing?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the guide or instructions did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about giving clear written directions or recommendations to a visitor in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during this writing-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement with peer feedback, clear instructions for the writing task, effective modeling of grammar points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more examples of complex sentence structures, dedicated time for vocabulary review before writing, clearer rubric for peer assessment). I noticed some students struggled with combining past simple and present perfect; I will provide more guided practice with this structure next time.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The written guides and recommendations created today will serve as input for Lesson 5, where students will mediate by summarizing or orally presenting their guide to a new group, potentially adapting it for a different audience or language to consolidate information and practice 21st-century skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>